### `Boukensha::Repl` (`repl.rb`) — new

- **No `attr_reader` anywhere in the class** — every constructor-assigned ivar (`@context`, `@registry`, `@builder`, `@client`, `@logger`, `@task_settings`, `@max_iterations`, `@max_output_tokens`, `@config_dir`, `@provider`, `@model`, `@version`, `@api_key`, `@turn`) is Ruby-private. Per Confirmed Decision 1, all become leading-underscore Python attributes. `PROMPT`/`HELP` are Ruby constants (public), ported as public uppercase class attributes — same category as `Agent.MAX_ITERATIONS`/`WRAP_UP_OUTPUT_TOKENS`.
- **`start`**: prints the banner once, then loops forever: print `PROMPT` (no trailing newline) and flush, read one line from stdin. `nil` (true EOF) breaks immediately with no message. Otherwise `chomp.strip` the line; an empty result loops again (no message, no error). Then dispatch: `/exit`/`/quit` → print `"Goodbye."`, break. `/help` → print `HELP`, loop again. `/quiet` → `Boukensha.quiet!`, print status, loop again. `/loud` → `Boukensha.loud!`, print status, loop again. `/clear` → `@context.clear_messages!`, reset `@turn` to `0`, print status, loop again. Anything else → `run_turn(input)`.
- **`run_turn(input)`** (private): increments `@turn`, calls `@logger.turn(n: @turn)` (JSONL only, no stdout — see README drift #4), appends the input as a `:user` message to `@context`, **constructs a brand-new `Agent.new(...)` for this turn** (sharing `@context`/`@registry`/`@builder`/`@client`/`@logger`/`@task_settings`/`@max_iterations`/`@max_output_tokens` — every prior turn's `Agent` is discarded), calls `agent.run`, then `puts; puts result`. **The `Agent` is per-turn, not per-session** — `@iteration` resets to `0` and the `max_iterations` ceiling applies fresh every turn; only `@context`'s message list carries state across turns. Two rescues at method level: `LoopError` → print `"\n[error] #{message}"`, no crash (dead code — nothing raises `LoopError` anywhere in this codebase, same as every step since 05; ported faithfully anyway, matching the existing `rescue`). `ApiError` → print `"\n[error] API call failed: #{message}"`, no crash.
- **`banner`** (private): builds a heredoc string — see the literal captured output in Transcript 1/2 above for exact spacing/characters. Key details: `key_status` is `"✗ API key not set"` when `@api_key` is `nil` or blank, else `"✓ API key set"`. `provider_line` is `"#{provider || "default"} (#{model || "default"})  #{key_status}"`. `config_line` is `@config_dir` itself if that directory exists on disk, else `"#{@config_dir || "(default)"}  ✗ directory not found"`. `ver` is `@version || "?.?.?"`, and the version padding inside the box is `" " * (9 - ver.length)` spaces (4 spaces for `"0.8.0"`, confirmed against the live capture). The heredoc's literal leading blank line and trailing blank line are both part of the string (confirmed against Transcript 1: one blank line before `╔══...`, one blank line after the `/exit or /quit` line, before the first `boukensha> ` prompt).
- **`HELP`** (public constant): a 6-line block — see Transcript 1's `/help` output for the exact text, ending in exactly one trailing newline (no extra blank line, unlike `banner`).

### `Boukensha.repl` (`boukensha.rb`) — new

Structurally identical to `Boukensha.run` (see step 07's plan for the full annotated source) with these differences:
- No `task:` parameter.
- After building `ctx`/`registry`/running the `block`/selecting the backend/building `builder`/`client`/computing `effective_max_iterations`/`effective_max_output_tokens`/`logger` (all identical to `run`), it constructs `Repl.new(...)` (passing `config_dir: cfg.dir`, `provider: backend`, `model: model`, `version: VERSION`, `api_key: api_key` in addition to the shared wiring) and calls `.start` instead of `agent.run`.
- `rescue Interrupt; puts "\nInterrupted."` wraps the whole method body **in addition to** `ensure logger&.close` — Ctrl-C during the REPL prints a message and still runs the `ensure`. Python: `except KeyboardInterrupt: print("\nInterrupted.")` alongside the existing `finally: if logger is not None: logger.close()` pattern from `run()`.

